## Introduction
The kidney is a focal point of several physiologically crucial functions in the body like homeostasis, regulation of electrolyte concentrations, extracellular fluid volume, and blood pressure. Many of these functions are required constantly and are accomplished by mechanisms of filtration, re-absorption, and secretion, which take place in the nephron and require multiple specialized cell types. The prevalence of chronic kidney disease is estimated to be $8–16\%$ worldwide, presenting a serious and increasing public health problem [1]. Once nephrons are chronically damaged, kidneys rarely regain their function and deteriorate progressively towards end-stage renal disease.
The generation of renal cells and tissues from human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) may enable the development of regenerative therapies as well as precision models for analyzing disease pathogenesis and drug screening. This approach is confronted with high cellular complexity owing to 26 different cell types in the kidney [2]. However, the mammalian kidney arises rather autonomously from intermediate mesoderm (IM), which forms two interactive progenitors, the ureteric bud (UB) cells and the metanephric mesenchyme (MM) cells. The arborization of ureteric epithelium into the mature collecting duct system, and the terminal differentiation of mesenchyme into functional nephrons are dependent upon continued cell–cell interactions between the ureteric bud epithelium and metanephric mesenchyme [3]. As the organ originates from these two progenitors, the challenging complexity of recapitulating nephrogenesis in vitro may be overcome by deriving from hPSCs the MM and UB cell types.
Recently published studies suggest that human-induced PSC (hiPSCs) and embryonic stem cells (ESCs) can be induced into early renal precursor cell types in vitro, including intermediate mesoderm (IM) [4, 5], UB [6], and MM cells [7, 8]. The potency of these cells to form or integrate into tubular and nephron-like structures has been illustrated in reaggregation assays with murine embryonic kidney cells [6, 7] and after grafting in animal models [8–10]. The generation of renal organoids from human hiPSCs makes the in vitro modeling of human kidney a realistic option. However, most of these experiments required three-dimensional (3D)-organ culture, complementation with embryonic animal tissues, or animal-derived substances to direct the renal precursors to a terminally differentiated phenotype, a process which takes about 21–28 days starting from hPSCs [8, 11]. We anticipate that kidney modeling in vitro and cell-based kidney regeneration will require both, 3D-organoids as well as tailored combinations of specific renal cell types typical for functional renal subunits such as the glomerulus or the tubular system. Protocols for their efficient generation enabling cost-effective good manufacturing practice are thus required. We hypothesized that two-dimensional (2D) cultivation can provide the interactive cellular and signaling environment described in vivo, and at an early timepoint allows the derivation of many renal cell types making them accessible for drug testing, organ modeling, and selective applications in regenerative medicine.
Here, we describe an efficient chemically defined protocol for the generation of renal precursor cells in 6 days and terminally differentiated kidney cells in 2D culture within 14 days. To mirror human kidney ontogenesis in vitro, we screened an array of candidate factors to establish UB and MM cells and further develop them into podocytes, mesangial cells, proximal and distal-tubular epithelial cells, and collecting duct cells that together constitute the major cell types of the nephron. Thus, our protocol generates an inductive environment and offers means to manufacture several cell products relevant for studying development, maturation, response, and recovery from disease.
## Results
## Derivation and characterization of renal progenitors from hPSCs
## Inducing mesoderm and nephrogenesis
Our strategy to generate mesoderm-derived renal progenitors from hPSCs was based on screening of factors known to be active during the well-established developmental progression of cells through a primitive streak phase to a mesoderm stage followed by specification to the intermediate mesoderm with nephric potential. The balance of bone morphogenic protein (BMP) and activin/nodal signaling with canonical Wnt signaling has been known to specify the cell fate of hPSC-derived-nascent primitive streak into mesoderm or endoderm progenitors [12]. In addition, retinoic acid (RA) is a morphogen specifying the anterio-posterior axis, a caudalizing agent that influences HOX genes during embryonic development [13, 14]. We compared four different growth factor combinations for their ability to produce renal progenitors (Fig. S1). For convenience, the induction cocktails used are denoted with the abbreviations of the factors, for, e.g., AB4RA represents a combination of ActivinA, BMP4, and retinoic acid, respectively. The efficiency of induction was tested by the extent of disappearance of OCT4, marking the loss of pluripotency and the subsequent appearance of Brachyury $(\mathrm{T})^{+}$ cells representing a mesendodermal commitment of stem cells. SOX17 was used to identify and minimize nascent definitive endoderm cells as we aim to achieve maximum induction of mesoderm, characterized by SIX2, LHX1, OSR1, PAX2, WT1, and CDH1 (data shown for SIX2). Evaluation of the extent of differentiation by day 4 shows that the combination AB4RA was the most efficient combination inducing mesoderm (Fig. S2).
After specification of the IM, the metanephros arises when the mesenchyme surrounding the nephric duct epithelium is activated. To spur nephrogenesis further in vitro, we attempted to derive early stages of renal cell specification by supporting mesenchyme growth and branching morphogenesis. MM-derived GDNF was previously shown to promote UB differentiation and branching, and provide a positive feedback to MM cells [15, 16]. Therefore, in a second step, day-4 mesoderm cells were further exposed to GDNF (AB4RA-G). Completion of the 8-day treatment resulted in total loss of OCT4 confirming differentiation of hPSCs. AB4RA-G showed some remnant populations of $\mathrm{T}^{+}$ cells and the highest number of $\mathrm{SIX}2^{+}$ cells faring well among all the combinations tested (Fig. S2). AB4RA-G was identified in this screening to be highly effective in jointly inducing cells of renal competence.
## Generation of renal progenitors by AB4RA‑G treatment for 8 days
The pattern of differentiation of hPSCs was accompanied by marked morphological changes of the cells. AB4RA after 2 days induced cell spreading accompanied by proliferation and considerable amount of cell death. Following 2 further days of AB4RA, the differentiating cells began forming dome-like structures (Fig. 1a). Removal of AB4RA and addition of GDNF (AB4RA-G) for the next 4 days lead to a loss of dome-like structures and cessation of massive cell death. We observed a heterogeneous cell mass characterized by epithelial islands surrounded by mesenchymal cells by day 8 of differentiation (Fig. 1a).
The developmental progression and identity of the different stages along the renal lineage after exposure of PSC to AB4RA-G were corroborated by RNA sequencing (RNAseq) at 2-day intervals (Fig. 2a). Activin A and BMP4 (AB4) induced intermediate mesoderm and meso-nephric genes by day 2 including OSR1, SIX2, PAX2, and PAX8 [17, 18] by day 4, confirming the immunofluorescence results during initial screening of factors (Fig. 2a).
## Progression to renal vesicle stage
Closer analysis of RNA-seq data indicated that there is a distinct transition from mesenchymal to epithelial states between day 6 and day 8, triggering a renal vesicle signature via the pre-tubular aggregate (PTA) formation at the end of day 8 (Fig. 2b). AB4RA induced metanephric markers such as SIX1, SIX2, SALL1, and CITED1 [17, 18] by day 4. The expression of several growth factors including FGF8, BMP7, and BMP2 highest on day 8 along with the WNTs secreted by the UB population. The observed elevation in the level of BMP7 at day 6 provides a differentiation signal to the $\mathrm{CITED1^{+}}$ cells, thereby depleting the nephron progenitor population for further maturation by exposing them to WNT/ß-catenin [19]. Concurrent with the sequence of embryonic events, we also observe a shift from ­CITED1+ cells at day 6 (CM) to the $\mathrm{LEF1^{+}/W N T4^{+}/}$ ${\mathrm{FGF}}8^{+}$ population representative of the PTA by day 8. The presence of RET at day 4 (Fig. 2b) hints at the readiness of the LHX-PAX2-expressing cells to receive the GDNF signal and activate UB formation indicated by the gradual appearance of CDH1 and SOX9 at day 6 that is prolonged until day 8. The highest expression of WNT11 and WNT9b at day 8 indicates the ability of UB cells to produce signals to regulate itself and the surrounding mesenchyme as is seen in vivo. RNA-seq data confirm a rise in WNTs and other WNT-associated proteins like LEF1, AXIN2 (Fig. 2b) and CTNNB (Fig. 1b) by day 8, associated with the epithelialization of renal progenitors. The presumptive PTA also expressed several genes associated with stage I nephrons—the renal vesicle (RV). Broadly, markers common to the proximal and distal RV like JAG1, CDH6, WNT4, POU3F3, and NPY showed elevated expression at day 8 [20]. Many of these genes are critical for specification of nephron segments, leading us to investigate protein localization of genes WT1 (marks future renal corpuscle), JAG1 (marks future proximal tubule), and LGR5 (marks future distal convoluted tubule), in day-8 cells (Fig. 2c). Indeed, their presence was detected but with no particular pattern of organization, as observed in vivo. We observed a cytoplasmic and membranous distribution of WT1 instead of a nuclear localization, which has been recorded previously for certain splice variants of WT1 [21].
## Efficacy of generating renal progenitors
The AB4RA-G was applied to three different hiPSC lines, the fibroblast-derived line WISCi004-A, and two urinary-cell-derived lines BCRTi004-A and BCRTi005- A [22, 23]. WISCi004-A showed $60\%$ efficiency in producing $\mathrm{SIX}2^{+}$ cells after 8 days of AB4RA-G treatment, while BCRTi004-A and BCRTi005-A attained $75\mathrm{-}80\%$ efficiency (Fig. 3a). The distribution of cells forming the MM, UB, and stromal compartments on day 8 was rather similar, but looking deeper into the distribution of these fractions at day 4 and day 6 reveals the predominance of MM marked by SIX2, taken over by FOXD1 expressing stromal cells over the course of the last 2 days (Fig. 1d). Occurrence of LHX1 in a majority of the cells at the day-8 stage corresponds to the manifestation of pre-tubular aggregate-like characteristics [20] in conjunction with PAX2, which is also representative of renal vesicle cells (Fig. 3b). On the other hand, association of LHX1 cells and CDH16 demonstrates the synchronous development of the ureteric epithelium (Fig. 3b), all at higher frequencies in the urinary-cell-derived hiPSC lines [24, 25]. Marked variances were observed in the potential of the three-hPSC lines used in this study to form RV (Fig. 3b). To understand the distribution of day-8 cells contributing to the renal vesicle and developing nephron compartments, we quantified the cells expressing JAG1 (marks future proximal tubule), LGR5 (marks future distal convoluted tubule) and FZD7 (marks all cells undergoing M.E.T in the human fetal kidney [26]). In Fig. 3c, line BCRTi005-A shows a balanced RV state of proximal and distal compartments, whereas both BCRTi004-A and WISCi004-A show a bias towards proximal RV, the latter being more efficient.
## Potency of RV‑like cells for nephron specification
Our analysis of day-8 cells indicated their committed state for nephronal development. To elucidate their inherent potency to generate different lineages of the nephron, day-8 RV-like cells were allowed to differentiate in the absence of induction factors post-dissociation. Pellets of dissociated day-8 cells were prepared and placed on a trans-well membrane, an air–liquid interface. Five days of pellet culture resulted in a 3D network of cells, which showed the presence of transporters characteristic of the different tubular compartments of the nephron (Fig. 3d). Enriched occurrence of AQP1 and $\mathrm{{Na^{+}/K^{+}}}$ -ATPase indicated the presence of proximal-tubule-like cells. In addition, SLC12A3 (sodium
