stage, by the addition of GDNF. This protocol is highly efficient, resulting in nephrogenic cells of which $60{-}80\%$ expressed the transcription factor SIX2, characteristic of the MM in three different hiPSC lines. About $30\mathrm{-}60\%$ of the day-8 cells were characteristic for CDH16 expressing UB cells. A third step steered the renal progenitors towards terminal differentiation, an evidence of their potency to generate kidney cells representative of the major nephron segments. These include glomerular podocyte-like cells and mesangial cells, proximal and distal-tubular epitheliallike cells, and collecting duct principle-like cells. The derivation of UB in 4 days [6], MM in 14 days [8], and both MM and UB in 18 days [11] has been reported by other groups. We see the appearance of MM and UB as early as 4 days and the induction of MM to cap mesenchyme by day
6, which by far is the fastest induction of nephrogenesis reported to date. Takasato et al. also observed the presence of RV on day 18, as compared to our protocol that takes just 8 days to show a polarized RV state. The uniqueness of our protocol is the use of GDNF, allowing the ureteric epithelial cells to develop and autonomous secretion of WNT11 and WNT9B to support ureteric elongation and
MM maintenance and activation. The data thus illustrate the effectiveness of paracrine signaling and gradient formation within the cell culture system in response to growth factor treatment.
Although human kidney 3D—organoids are highly relevant as models for drug testing and disease modeling, or to generate bioengineered kidneys for regenerative therapies, the generation of defined cell types as shown enables cell-specific analysis by high-throughput and simple 2D assays. Large numbers of multiple defined renal cell types will be required for bio-printing of renal structures, to repopulate decellularized matrices, or for high-throughput drug testing as well as disease modeling. Other protocols for direct differentiation of PSC into tubular epithelial cells [29] and podocytes [30] have been shown, but these can generate only a single-cell type. Using these methods, isolation and standardized establishment of specific kidney cell types for therapeutic or modeling purposes is tedious. Another viable option is the use of human fetal kidney cells [26] that are expanded in vitro; nevertheless, this is a limiting source. The generation of these renal cell types from a single precursor pool, which is available at a maximum of 8 days by
2D-culture makes our protocol attractive for standardized up-scaling. Although existing 2D-expansion technology may be applicable, the development of optimized materials and media for growth of renal precursor cell populations and their rapid and specific differentiation into terminal cell types will be required. Brown et al. developed a nephron progenitor expansion medium (N.P.E.M); application of this medium on hPSC-derived nephron progenitors (Takasato protocol) showed their critical dependence on WNT, FGF9, SMAD inhibition, and Rho-kinase inhibition, and allowed their expansion for 2 passages after which cells lost their identity [31]. A more recent report shows that NOTCH inhibition and LIF are essential in maintaining nephron progenitors that retain potency to form both tubules and glomeruli in embryonic spinal cord recombination experiments [32]. On the other hand, when hPSC-derived renal progenitors are accomplished in NPEM for kidney regeneration, it may generate heterogenous cultures from which NPCs can be prospectively sorted out according to specific markers described in human fetal kidney-derived populations described by Pode-Shakked et al. [9, 33]. These studies present a first step in expansion and modulation of the fate of nephron progenitors in vitro presenting the possibility of expansion and testing on various extracellular matrix scaffolds for generation of kidney tissues. Moreover, our data confirm that variability between different PSC lines needs to be considered for up-scaling. It is noteworthy that the two urinary-cell-derived hiPSC cell lines were more efficiently differentiated into the renal lineage than the fibroblast-derived line.
As kidneys rarely regenerate after damage and endogenous repair is limited to the tubular epithelium and effective only in acute kidney injury, chronic kidney disease is left with very few therapeutic options, including dialysis and kidney transplantation. These limitations have led to a quest for alternative therapies to regenerate the damaged kidney by either stimulating endogenous regeneration or by bioengineering a kidney [32]. Direct therapeutic application of renal precursor cells in kidney regeneration has been attempted using renal stem and precursor cells [34], adult stem [9, 35], or hPSC –derived renal cell types [36, 37] in animal models. The outcomes of these studies encourage the development of prototypes for cellbased regenerative therapy in humans, where large cell numbers of differentiated and precursor renal cells will be required. The addition of hPSC-derived nephron cell types into three-dimensional environments in polymer based scaffolds or acellular matrix of kidneys can help accomplish maturation and integration by providing spatial cues that define cellular identity.
## Materials and methods
## Ethical and legal statement
For animal experiments, all animal experiments were performed under the guidelines of the German Animal Protection Law with approval of the local responsible authorities (Landesamt für Gesundheit und Soziales Berlin—Permit number T 0084/15).
## Pluripotent stem cell culture
The hiPSC lines WISCi004-A (also referred to as IMR90-4 iPS derived from female lung fibroblasts) (WiCell) between 35 and 65 passages, BCRTi004-A [22], and BCRTi005-A [23] derived from urinary cells at passages 25–30 and hESC line WAe001-A (WA01) (WiCell), at passages 50–65 were used in this study. Experiments were performed on all 4 lines until 10 days of differentiation, while further specification was done with WISCi004-A. The stem cells were expanded under feeder-free culture conditions in mTeSR-1 or TeSR-E8 medium (Stemcell Technologies) on Geltrex (Life technologies)-coated dishes. Cultures were fed daily and passaged every 4–6 days with gentle cell dissociation reagent (Stemcell Technologies) for 5 min at room temperature and then manually detached from the dish using a cell scraper. The resulting clumps of cells were plated in a ratio of 1:6.
## Renal differentiation
Confluent hPSCs were dissociated with Accutase (Life Technologies) and $\ensuremath{10,000}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cells}}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ seeded in stem cell culture medium containing $10\upmu\mathrm{M}\ Y27632\left(\mathrm{Wako}\right)$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . At $75\%$ confluency, induction factors were applied in STEMdiff APEL Medium (Stemcell Technologies) for specified time periods (Fig. 6). During Stage I, $10\mathrm{ng/ml}$ Activin A (Peprotech), $30\mathrm{ng/ml}$ recombinant human (rh) BMP-4 (Peprotech) with either $1\upmu\mathrm{M}$ all-trans-retinoic acid (RA; Sigma-Aldrich) or $10~\mathrm{{ng/ml}}$ FGF2 (Peprotech) or $15\mathrm{ng/ml}$ rh-BMP7 (Peprotech) or $3~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ $(2^{\prime}Z,3^{\prime}E)$ -6-bromoindirubin- $3^{\prime}$ -oxime (BIO; Sigma-Aldrich) was added to APEL base. For stage II medium (days 4–8), $150~\mathrm{ng/ml}$ GDNF (Peprotech) or alternatively a combination of $15\mathrm{ng/ml}$ rhBMP7 and $10\mathrm{ng/}$ ml bFGF was added. During the course of differentiation, medium was replenished every $48\mathrm{h}$ (Fig. S1). For terminal differentiation, WISCi004-A cells obtained at the end of day 8 were re-plated on Laminin521 (BioLamina) with $50\mathrm{ng/ml}$ rhHGF (Peprotech) for podocyte differentiation or alternatively REGM (Lonza) for tubular epithelial differentiation and in a medium containing $15~\mathrm{{ng/ml}}$ rhBMP7 and $10~\mathrm{{ng/}}$ ml FGF2 for mesangial cells.
## Gene expression analysis
Total RNA was extracted from WISCi004-A hiPSCs and WISCi004-A-derived cells on days 2, 4, 6, 8, and 14 of our differentiation protocol, using RNeasy Mini kit (Qiagen). Contaminating DNA was degraded with the use of the DNase I, RNase-free kit (Fermentas, EN0521). For Illumina HiSeq 2000 RNA sequencing, RNA-seq library preparation and NGS: cDNA libraries from poly-A-tailenriched RNA were prepared from 3 biological replicates for each timepoint, using TruSeq mRNA sample prep kit v.2 (Illumina). Sequence alignment and RNA-Seq analysis: Bcl-to-fastq conversion was performed using the Illumina software (Illumina). Fastq files were aligned to build 19 of the human genome provided by the Genome Reference Consortium (GRCh19). Transcript alignment was performed using TopHat Analysis [38] of differential expression and transcript abundance was performed using Cuffdiff [39] from the Cufflinks analysis package (version 2.1.1). The transcript levels (expressed as Fragments per Kilobase of transcript per Million mapped reads; FPKM) of the samples were used to plot heatmaps based on Pearson correlation, using R [40] (version 3.1 or greater).The data (significantly impacted pathways, biological processes, molecular interactions, miRNAs, SNPs, etc.) were also analyzed using Advaita Bio’s iPathwayGuide [41]. Data from RNA-seq are represented as mean FPKM value of triplicates obtained at every timepoint. The RNA-seq data set is publicly available at Gene Expression Omnibus (http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ geo) under the accession number GSE75711.
